# Authentication and authorization

Use the project's existing session, password, and login libraries. Do not add
a second identity stack. Floor controls (deny-by-default, object-level authz,
cookie flags, CSRF requirement) live in `security-baseline`. Browser CSRF and
header mechanics live in `security-transport`. JWT signature and algorithm
checks live in `security-crypto`.

## Sessions and cookies

- Set session cookies `HttpOnly` and `Secure`. Set `SameSite` to the strictest
  value that still lets the product's real cross-site flows work
- Rotate the session identifier on login and on privilege change
- Expire idle and absolute sessions. Invalidate server-side state on logout
- Do not put secrets or long-lived tokens in `localStorage` when a cookie
  session already exists
- Cookie-authenticated state changes need the cross-site request check from
  `security-transport`

## Passwords and recovery

- Hash passwords with a modern password hashing function from a maintained
  library. Store no plaintext and no reversible encryption of the password
- Recovery, invite, and magic-link tokens are single-use, time-limited, and
  high entropy. Consume them with a compare-and-swap or equivalent so two
  concurrent requests cannot both succeed
- Compare secrets in constant time
- When the product hides whether an account exists, failed login and recovery
  responses stay generic
- Rate-limit authentication and other expensive public endpoints when adding
  them. A new unlimited login route is a credential-stuffing target

## Federated login and bearer tokens

- Allowlist redirect targets. Validate `state` and, for OpenID, `nonce`
- Public clients use PKCE
- Keep access tokens short-lived. Rotate refresh tokens. Revoke them on logout
  and on suspected theft
- Do not put secrets in a token payload. A payload is readable
- Lifetime, storage, and revocation live here. Signature, algorithm allowlist,
  `none` rejection, and iss/aud/exp checks live in `security-crypto`

## Authorization

- Deny by default. Check the actor's right on this object, not only that the
  actor is signed in (object-level authorization)
- Enforce the check on the server for every state-changing and sensitive read
- Do not trust a role, tenant, or price sent by the client
- Changing an admin or a tenant boundary requires the same check as the
  action it guards
